3. BFS + Number of Shortest Paths

Now we want:

How many shortest paths exist from the source to each node?

Add:

int[] ways = new int[n + 1];

Initialize:

ways[start] = 1;

Why 1?

There is exactly one way to be at the starting node — start there.

Code
void bfs(int start, ArrayList<Integer>[] graph, int n) {

    boolean[] visited = new boolean[n + 1];

    int[] level = new int[n + 1];
    int[] ways = new int[n + 1];

    Queue<Integer> q = new LinkedList<>();

    q.add(start);

    visited[start] = true;
    level[start] = 0;
    ways[start] = 1;

    while(!q.isEmpty()) {

        int node = q.poll();

        for(int neighbour : graph[node]) {

            // First time reaching neighbour
            if(!visited[neighbour]) {

                visited[neighbour] = true;

                level[neighbour] = level[node] + 1;

                ways[neighbour] = ways[node];

                q.add(neighbour);
            }

            // Another shortest path to neighbour
            else if(level[neighbour] == level[node] + 1) {

                ways[neighbour] += ways[node];
            }
        }
    }
}
The two important cases

First time:

ways[neighbour] = ways[node];

Already visited, but another shortest path:

ways[neighbour] += ways[node];

Only if:

level[neighbour] == level[node] + 1
